              title: `Rwanda: Authoritarian Development After Genocide`,
              context: `Background for the genocide covered in L08 (1994 genocide killed ~800K-1M Tutsi and moderate Hutu in 100 days; Hutu Power organization; international failure; gacaca courts processed ~1.2M cases post-1994). Post-genocide trajectory: Paul Kagame's Rwandan Patriotic Front (RPF) military victory drove Hutu Power forces from the country (with approximately 2 million Hutu civilians fleeing into eastern Zaire/DRC, contributing to subsequent regional conflicts). Kagame served as Vice President and Minister of Defense from 1994 (Pasteur Bizimungu was nominal President); Kagame became President in 2000; re-elected with 95% (2003), 93% (2010); after 2015 constitutional amendments allowing extended tenure he was re-elected with 99% (2017) and 99% (2024); his current term extends through 2034. Achievements substantial and documented: 30+ years of stability after one of the worst genocides in history; economic growth averaging ~7% annually for sustained periods; GDP per capita rose from ~$200 (1995) to ~$1,000 (2023); life expectancy from ~28 (1994) to ~67 (2020s); under-5 mortality reduced ~75% since 1990s; maternal mortality reduced ~70%; school enrollment near universal at primary level; women's representation in parliament highest globally (~60%+ since 2013, currently ~61% in Lower House); Kigali has become reference point for urban planning, cleanliness, infrastructure across Africa; Vision 2050 development plan substantial; reconciliation process through gacaca community courts (2001-2012) processed ~1.2 million cases of genocide-era crimes through community-based justice. Challenges and criticisms equally substantial and documented: authoritarian rule with no meaningful electoral competition (presidential elections with 93-99% margins indicate substantial constraints on contestation); opposition figures imprisoned, exiled, or in some cases killed (Patrick Karegeya, former head of intelligence who broke with Kagame, found strangled in Johannesburg hotel December 31, 2013, with subsequent investigation identifying Rwandan intelligence involvement; Kayumba Nyamwasa, former army chief now exiled, survived multiple assassination attempts in South Africa; Diane Rwigara prosecuted multiple times after announcing presidential candidacy 2017; Paul Rusesabagina, hero of "Hotel Rwanda" 2004 film, imprisoned 2020-2023 on terrorism charges widely seen as politically motivated, released to US after diplomatic pressure); press freedom restricted (Rwanda ranks 144/180 on World Press Freedom Index 2024 per Reporters Without Borders); Hutu/Tutsi categories politically banned but persistent in practice (RPF substantially Tutsi-dominated; surveillance of Hutu population reported); RPF involvement in Eastern Congo conflicts (M23 rebellion in DRC 2012+ and 2022+ with substantial Rwandan military support documented in UN Group of Experts reports; sexual violence and displacement in eastern DRC; ~7 million displaced in DRC partly due to conflicts involving Rwandan-backed forces). Rwanda has actively projected influence: Commonwealth membership 2009 (despite no British colonial history); replaced French with English as official language 2008; Commonwealth Heads of Government Meeting hosted Kigali 2022; substantial diplomatic and military presence across Africa. The "Singapore of Africa" framing emphasized by Kagame and Western partners describes authoritarian-developmental model with substantial governance gains and substantial political restrictions.`,
              question: `What's the most important framing for understanding Rwanda's trajectory?`,
